1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,644
It's October thirty-first,

2
00:00:01,644 --> 00:00:02,972
nineteen fifty-eight.

3
00:00:03,152 --> 00:00:05,107
The sun is going down early,

4
00:00:05,107 --> 00:00:07,830
and the air smells like burning leaves.

5
00:00:08,010 --> 00:00:09,758
On every street in town,

6
00:00:09,758 --> 00:00:12,306
front porch lights are clicking on.

7
00:00:12,486 --> 00:00:15,104
Inside, kids are wriggling into costumes,

8
00:00:15,104 --> 00:00:17,275
and mothers are trying to get them

9
00:00:17,275 --> 00:00:19,127
to eat a little supper first.

10
00:00:19,307 --> 00:00:21,049
Nobody is listening.

11
00:00:21,229 --> 00:00:23,121
Tonight is Halloween.

12
00:00:23,471 --> 00:00:25,435
If you were a child in nineteen

13
00:00:25,435 --> 00:00:28,413
fifty-eight, Halloween might have been the best

14
00:00:28,413 --> 00:00:29,934
night of the whole year,

15
00:00:29,934 --> 00:00:30,947
after Christmas.

16
00:00:31,127 --> 00:00:32,718
No school the next day?

17
00:00:32,898 --> 00:00:33,792
Not usually.

18
00:00:33,972 --> 00:00:35,301
But for a few hours,

19
00:00:35,301 --> 00:00:37,161
you could go out after dark,

20
00:00:37,161 --> 00:00:39,353
knock on every door on the block,

21
00:00:39,353 --> 00:00:41,745
and come home with a pillowcase full

22
00:00:41,745 --> 00:00:42,343
of candy.

23
00:00:42,693 --> 00:00:45,810
So tonight, let's go trick-or-treating in nineteen

24
00:00:45,810 --> 00:00:46,559
fifty-eight.

25
00:00:46,739 --> 00:00:47,825
What we wore,

26
00:00:47,825 --> 00:00:48,827
what we got,

27
00:00:48,827 --> 00:00:50,164
how far we went,

28
00:00:50,164 --> 00:00:53,088
and why Halloween back then felt so

29
00:00:53,088 --> 00:00:54,842
different from today.

30
00:00:55,022 --> 00:00:56,120
If you were there,

31
00:00:56,120 --> 00:00:58,621
I think you'll remember that cool October

32
00:00:58,621 --> 00:01:00,084
air the moment we start.

33
00:01:00,435 --> 00:01:03,174
Here's something many people don't realize.

34
00:01:03,354 --> 00:01:04,876
In nineteen fifty-eight,

35
00:01:04,876 --> 00:01:07,348
trick-or-treating was still fairly new.

36
00:01:07,528 --> 00:01:10,272
The phrase "trick or treat" only became

37
00:01:10,272 --> 00:01:13,226
common across America in the late nineteen

38
00:01:13,226 --> 00:01:14,703
thirties and forties.

39
00:01:14,883 --> 00:01:17,785
Before that, Halloween was better known for

40
00:01:17,785 --> 00:01:20,214
pranks, and some of them were pretty

41
00:01:20,214 --> 00:01:20,619
rough.

42
00:01:20,969 --> 00:01:22,933
Boys tipped over outhouses,

43
00:01:22,933 --> 00:01:25,407
soaped windows, let the air out of

44
00:01:25,407 --> 00:01:28,318
tires and dragged gates into the street.

45
00:01:28,498 --> 00:01:29,551
In some towns,

46
00:01:29,551 --> 00:01:31,883
the damage got so bad that city

47
00:01:31,883 --> 00:01:35,268
leaders decided to give kids something better

48
00:01:35,268 --> 00:01:35,719
to do.

49
00:01:35,899 --> 00:01:39,516
Parades, parties and door-to-door treats were the

50
00:01:39,516 --> 00:01:40,032
answer.

51
00:01:40,212 --> 00:01:42,056
A treat, in other words,

52
00:01:42,056 --> 00:01:44,360
was a polite bribe to skip the

53
00:01:44,360 --> 00:01:44,821
trick.

54
00:01:45,172 --> 00:01:47,424
During the Second World War,

55
00:01:47,424 --> 00:01:48,952
sugar was rationed,

56
00:01:48,952 --> 00:01:51,847
and trick-or-treating mostly paused.

57
00:01:52,027 --> 00:01:53,215
But after the war,

58
00:01:53,215 --> 00:01:55,393
with sugar back and the baby boom

59
00:01:55,393 --> 00:01:57,702
filling every street with children,

60
00:01:57,702 --> 00:01:58,494
it took off.

61
00:01:58,674 --> 00:01:59,951
By the late fifties,

62
00:01:59,951 --> 00:02:01,739
it was a national tradition.

63
00:02:01,919 --> 00:02:06,043
Television shows, magazines and even cereal boxes

64
00:02:06,043 --> 00:02:08,990
all showed kids going door to door.

65
00:02:09,339 --> 00:02:10,988
The costume came first.

66
00:02:11,168 --> 00:02:13,026
And in nineteen fifty-eight,

67
00:02:13,026 --> 00:02:14,419
there were two kinds.

68
00:02:14,599 --> 00:02:16,102
The homemade costume,

69
00:02:16,102 --> 00:02:18,535
and the store-bought one in a box.

70
00:02:18,715 --> 00:02:21,373
The homemade ones were usually the best.

71
00:02:21,553 --> 00:02:24,126
A white bedsheet with two eyeholes made

72
00:02:24,126 --> 00:02:24,653
a ghost.

73
00:02:24,833 --> 00:02:26,965
A burnt cork rubbed on the face

74
00:02:26,965 --> 00:02:29,233
made a hobo with a stubble beard.

75
00:02:29,583 --> 00:02:31,395
Mothers stayed up late sewing.

76
00:02:31,575 --> 00:02:33,829
Cowboy outfits, pirates,

77
00:02:33,829 --> 00:02:36,834
princesses, witches and fairies.

78
00:02:37,014 --> 00:02:39,467
Old clothes from the attic turned into

79
00:02:39,467 --> 00:02:40,242
a scarecrow.

80
00:02:40,422 --> 00:02:43,092
A cardboard box became a robot.

81
00:02:43,272 --> 00:02:45,203
And for a few years after the

82
00:02:45,203 --> 00:02:46,201
movie came out,

83
00:02:46,201 --> 00:02:48,997
every neighborhood seemed to have at least

84
00:02:48,997 --> 00:02:51,527
one little Davy Crockett in a coonskin

85
00:02:51,527 --> 00:02:51,794
cap.

86
00:02:52,143 --> 00:02:55,841
The store-bought costumes came from companies like

87
00:02:55,841 --> 00:02:57,912
Ben Cooper and Collegeville,

88
00:02:57,912 --> 00:03:00,722
sold at the five-and-dime for a couple

89
00:03:00,722 --> 00:03:01,536
of dollars.

90
00:03:01,716 --> 00:03:04,094
They came in a thin cardboard box

91
00:03:04,094 --> 00:03:05,896
with a cellophane window.

92
00:03:06,076 --> 00:03:08,792
Inside was a flimsy vinyl suit that

93
00:03:08,792 --> 00:03:10,112
tied in the back,

94
00:03:10,112 --> 00:03:12,518
and a hard plastic mask held on

95
00:03:12,518 --> 00:03:14,226
by a thin rubber band.

96
00:03:14,576 --> 00:03:16,201
You remember that mask.

97
00:03:16,381 --> 00:03:18,440
It had tiny eyeholes you could barely

98
00:03:18,440 --> 00:03:20,332
see through, and a little slit for

99
00:03:20,332 --> 00:03:20,944
your mouth.

100
00:03:21,124 --> 00:03:22,406
After ten minutes,

101
00:03:22,406 --> 00:03:24,900
your breath made the inside wet and

102
00:03:24,900 --> 00:03:25,257
warm.

103
00:03:25,437 --> 00:03:27,434
The rubber band pulled your hair.

104
00:03:27,614 --> 00:03:29,187
And by the third house,

105
00:03:29,187 --> 00:03:31,377
most kids had pushed the mask up

106
00:03:31,377 --> 00:03:33,361
on top of their heads anyway.

107
00:03:33,711 --> 00:03:34,860
Next came the bag.

108
00:03:35,040 --> 00:03:38,017
Some kids carried a paper grocery sack,

109
00:03:38,017 --> 00:03:40,613
which worked fine until it rained.

110
00:03:40,793 --> 00:03:44,055
Others had a plastic jack-o'-lantern pail.

111
00:03:44,235 --> 00:03:47,741
But the serious trick-or-treaters carried a pillowcase.

112
00:03:47,921 --> 00:03:50,032
A pillowcase could hold a lot of

113
00:03:50,032 --> 00:03:51,880
candy, and a kid with a full

114
00:03:51,880 --> 00:03:54,123
pillowcase was a kid who knew what

115
00:03:54,123 --> 00:03:54,980
he was doing.

116
00:03:55,331 --> 00:03:58,328
Many kids also carried a little orange

117
00:03:58,328 --> 00:04:00,300
and black box for UNICEF.

118
00:04:00,480 --> 00:04:03,816
The Trick-or-Treat for UNICEF program started in

119
00:04:03,816 --> 00:04:06,040
nineteen fifty, in Philadelphia,

120
00:04:06,040 --> 00:04:09,099
when a group of children collected seventeen

121
00:04:09,099 --> 00:04:11,184
dollars for children overseas.

122
00:04:11,364 --> 00:04:12,823
By the late fifties,

123
00:04:12,823 --> 00:04:14,136
it was nationwide,

124
00:04:14,136 --> 00:04:16,105
and kids collected pennies,

125
00:04:16,105 --> 00:04:19,096
nickels and dimes along with their candy.

126
00:04:19,447 --> 00:04:20,900
Before anyone went out,

127
00:04:20,900 --> 00:04:22,291
there was the pumpkin.

128
00:04:22,471 --> 00:04:25,081
Fathers usually handled the big knife,

129
00:04:25,081 --> 00:04:27,760
while the kids scooped out the stringy,

130
00:04:27,760 --> 00:04:29,820
slippery insides with a spoon.

131
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:31,847
Mothers saved the seeds,

132
00:04:31,847 --> 00:04:33,771
washed them, salted them,

133
00:04:33,771 --> 00:04:36,002
and roasted them in the oven.

134
00:04:36,182 --> 00:04:39,143
The whole kitchen smelled like warm pumpkin.

135
00:04:39,493 --> 00:04:40,944
The faces were simple.

136
00:04:41,124 --> 00:04:43,616
Triangle eyes, a triangle nose,

137
00:04:43,616 --> 00:04:45,947
and a jagged smile with a few

138
00:04:45,947 --> 00:04:46,430
teeth.

139
00:04:46,610 --> 00:04:48,743
Then a real candle went inside,

140
00:04:48,743 --> 00:04:50,464
lit with a kitchen match.

141
00:04:50,644 --> 00:04:53,516
The jack-o'-lantern sat on the porch step,

142
00:04:53,516 --> 00:04:55,089
flickering in the dark,

143
00:04:55,089 --> 00:04:57,004
and by the end of the night,

144
00:04:57,004 --> 00:04:59,398
the top was usually scorched black.

145
00:04:59,748 --> 00:05:01,675
And then, out the door.

146
00:05:01,855 --> 00:05:03,817
Here's the biggest difference from today.

147
00:05:03,997 --> 00:05:05,643
In nineteen fifty-eight,

148
00:05:05,643 --> 00:05:08,455
most kids went out without their parents.

149
00:05:08,635 --> 00:05:11,301
Older brothers and sisters took the little

150
00:05:11,301 --> 00:05:11,619
ones.

151
00:05:11,799 --> 00:05:14,117
Kids traveled in packs of five,

152
00:05:14,117 --> 00:05:14,939
six or ten,

153
00:05:14,939 --> 00:05:17,630
and covered blocks and blocks of the

154
00:05:17,630 --> 00:05:18,602
neighborhood.

155
00:05:18,782 --> 00:05:21,397
A ten-year-old might be gone for two

156
00:05:21,397 --> 00:05:22,486
or three hours.

157
00:05:22,836 --> 00:05:24,205
The rules were simple.

158
00:05:24,385 --> 00:05:25,546
Stay together.

159
00:05:25,726 --> 00:05:27,874
Only go to houses with the porch

160
00:05:27,874 --> 00:05:28,478
light on.

161
00:05:28,658 --> 00:05:29,912
Say thank you.

162
00:05:30,092 --> 00:05:31,258
Be home by eight,

163
00:05:31,258 --> 00:05:33,041
or nine if you were older.

164
00:05:33,221 --> 00:05:35,321
And don't eat anything until you get

165
00:05:35,321 --> 00:05:35,613
home.

166
00:05:35,793 --> 00:05:39,419
That last rule was broken almost immediately

167
00:05:39,419 --> 00:05:41,563
by every child in America.

168
00:05:41,913 --> 00:05:44,443
Every neighborhood had its famous houses.

169
00:05:44,623 --> 00:05:47,423
The house that gave full-size candy bars,

170
00:05:47,423 --> 00:05:49,744
which you visited once in costume,

171
00:05:49,744 --> 00:05:51,928
and then again with your mask on

172
00:05:51,928 --> 00:05:52,611
backwards.

173
00:05:52,791 --> 00:05:55,229
The house with the scary decorations.

174
00:05:55,409 --> 00:05:57,308
The old lady who made you come

175
00:05:57,308 --> 00:05:58,765
inside and sing a song.

176
00:05:58,945 --> 00:06:01,127
And the dark house where nobody went.

177
00:06:01,478 --> 00:06:03,686
And there was always a little danger

178
00:06:03,686 --> 00:06:04,360
in the air,

179
00:06:04,360 --> 00:06:05,158
the fun kind.

180
00:06:05,338 --> 00:06:07,805
A big kid jumping out from behind

181
00:06:07,805 --> 00:06:08,403
a hedge.

182
00:06:08,583 --> 00:06:10,684
A dog barking in the dark.

183
00:06:10,864 --> 00:06:13,560
A dad sitting perfectly still on his

184
00:06:13,560 --> 00:06:15,732
porch dressed as a scarecrow,

185
00:06:15,732 --> 00:06:16,855
until he moved.

186
00:06:17,035 --> 00:06:18,557
Kids screamed, ran,

187
00:06:18,557 --> 00:06:21,522
laughed, and then went right back for

188
00:06:21,522 --> 00:06:21,923
more.

189
00:06:22,273 --> 00:06:23,840
So what went into the bag?

190
00:06:24,020 --> 00:06:25,179
In the fifties,

191
00:06:25,179 --> 00:06:28,037
much of it was homemade or unwrapped.

192
00:06:28,217 --> 00:06:31,390
Popcorn balls held together with syrup and

193
00:06:31,390 --> 00:06:32,977
wrapped in wax paper.

194
00:06:33,157 --> 00:06:35,166
Caramel apples on a stick.

195
00:06:35,346 --> 00:06:38,190
Homemade fudge, cookies and brownies.

196
00:06:38,370 --> 00:06:40,135
Neighbors knew each other,

197
00:06:40,135 --> 00:06:42,715
so nobody thought twice about eating a

198
00:06:42,715 --> 00:06:43,734
treat from Mrs.

199
00:06:43,914 --> 00:06:45,400
Johnson down the street.

200
00:06:45,750 --> 00:06:47,766
There was also loose candy,

201
00:06:47,766 --> 00:06:49,334
often from big bowls,

202
00:06:49,334 --> 00:06:51,276
handed out by the handful.

203
00:06:51,456 --> 00:06:52,945
Candy corn, of course,

204
00:06:52,945 --> 00:06:55,653
which had been around since the eighteen

205
00:06:55,653 --> 00:06:56,262
eighties.

206
00:06:56,442 --> 00:06:57,650
Tootsie Rolls.

207
00:06:57,830 --> 00:06:59,269
Dubble Bubble gum.

208
00:06:59,449 --> 00:07:02,258
Wax lips and those little wax bottles

209
00:07:02,258 --> 00:07:04,839
with a sip of sweet colored liquid

210
00:07:04,839 --> 00:07:05,371
inside.

211
00:07:05,551 --> 00:07:09,417
Mary Janes, Bit-O-Honey and Necco wafers.

212
00:07:09,767 --> 00:07:12,681
Some houses gave apples or oranges,

213
00:07:12,681 --> 00:07:17,012
which kids politely accepted and secretly considered

214
00:07:17,012 --> 00:07:18,428
a waste of space.

215
00:07:18,608 --> 00:07:19,945
Some gave pennies,

216
00:07:19,945 --> 00:07:21,208
or even a nickel.

217
00:07:21,388 --> 00:07:23,868
And a few lucky houses gave real

218
00:07:23,868 --> 00:07:24,720
candy bars.

219
00:07:24,900 --> 00:07:26,336
A Hershey bar,

220
00:07:26,336 --> 00:07:27,567
a Baby Ruth,

221
00:07:27,567 --> 00:07:28,797
a Milky Way,

222
00:07:28,797 --> 00:07:31,054
or a Three Musketeers.

223
00:07:31,234 --> 00:07:32,960
In nineteen fifty-eight,

224
00:07:32,960 --> 00:07:35,262
a candy bar cost about a nickel.

225
00:07:35,613 --> 00:07:37,935
Halloween wasn't just trick-or-treating.

226
00:07:38,115 --> 00:07:40,124
Schools held costume parades,

227
00:07:40,124 --> 00:07:43,449
and children marched around the playground while

228
00:07:43,449 --> 00:07:44,558
parents watched.

229
00:07:44,738 --> 00:07:48,593
Churches and community centers held Halloween parties.

230
00:07:48,773 --> 00:07:51,844
And many families threw parties at home,

231
00:07:51,844 --> 00:07:55,223
in basements decorated with orange and black

232
00:07:55,223 --> 00:07:56,145
crepe paper.

233
00:07:56,495 --> 00:07:58,748
The games were always the same,

234
00:07:58,748 --> 00:07:59,838
and always fun.

235
00:08:00,018 --> 00:08:02,468
Bobbing for apples in a washtub full

236
00:08:02,468 --> 00:08:03,421
of cold water,

237
00:08:03,421 --> 00:08:04,986
hands behind your back,

238
00:08:04,986 --> 00:08:07,437
until your face and your collar were

239
00:08:07,437 --> 00:08:07,913
soaked.

240
00:08:08,093 --> 00:08:10,125
Pin the tail on the black cat.

241
00:08:10,305 --> 00:08:12,395
A haunted house in the basement,

242
00:08:12,395 --> 00:08:14,354
where you stuck your hand in a

243
00:08:14,354 --> 00:08:16,966
bowl of cold spaghetti and somebody told

244
00:08:16,966 --> 00:08:18,141
you it was brains.

245
00:08:18,492 --> 00:08:20,575
Grown-ups had their own parties,

246
00:08:20,575 --> 00:08:23,375
with punch, doughnuts and fresh apple cider

247
00:08:23,375 --> 00:08:24,482
from the orchard.

248
00:08:24,662 --> 00:08:25,979
In farm country,

249
00:08:25,979 --> 00:08:28,942
there were hayrides under the stars,

250
00:08:28,942 --> 00:08:31,164
barn dances and corn mazes.

251
00:08:31,344 --> 00:08:33,109
And in many small towns,

252
00:08:33,109 --> 00:08:36,272
the whole community gathered for a bonfire,

253
00:08:36,272 --> 00:08:38,553
a parade down Main Street and a

254
00:08:38,553 --> 00:08:40,539
prize for the best costume.

255
00:08:40,889 --> 00:08:42,843
And in nineteen fifty-eight,

256
00:08:42,843 --> 00:08:44,937
Halloween had a new companion:

257
00:08:44,937 --> 00:08:46,403
horror on television.

258
00:08:46,583 --> 00:08:48,342
In nineteen fifty-seven,

259
00:08:48,342 --> 00:08:51,641
a package of classic Universal monster movies

260
00:08:51,641 --> 00:08:54,206
called Shock Theater was sold to TV

261
00:08:54,206 --> 00:08:56,405
stations all over the country.

262
00:08:56,585 --> 00:09:00,213
Suddenly Dracula, Frankenstein and the Wolf Man

263
00:09:00,213 --> 00:09:02,837
were showing up in living rooms on

264
00:09:02,837 --> 00:09:04,921
Friday and Saturday nights.

265
00:09:05,272 --> 00:09:08,748
Local stations hired horror hosts to introduce

266
00:09:08,748 --> 00:09:09,579
the movies.

267
00:09:09,759 --> 00:09:12,471
Men and women in capes and fake

268
00:09:12,471 --> 00:09:15,359
fangs, with names like Zacherley,

269
00:09:15,359 --> 00:09:18,072
Vampira and Sir Graves Ghastly.

270
00:09:18,252 --> 00:09:19,447
Kids loved them.

271
00:09:19,627 --> 00:09:20,922
They stayed up late,

272
00:09:20,922 --> 00:09:22,282
hid behind the couch,

273
00:09:22,282 --> 00:09:24,548
and then lay awake in bed listening

274
00:09:24,548 --> 00:09:26,361
to every creak in the house.

275
00:09:26,712 --> 00:09:29,747
Nineteen fifty-eight also gave us a hit

276
00:09:29,747 --> 00:09:30,136
song.

277
00:09:30,316 --> 00:09:32,859
That summer, The Purple People Eater by

278
00:09:32,859 --> 00:09:35,010
Sheb Wooley went to number one on

279
00:09:35,010 --> 00:09:35,727
the charts.

280
00:09:35,907 --> 00:09:38,251
Another monster novelty song,

281
00:09:38,251 --> 00:09:39,625
The Monster Mash,

282
00:09:39,625 --> 00:09:42,455
would follow in nineteen sixty-two.

283
00:09:42,635 --> 00:09:44,527
Monsters, it turned out,

284
00:09:44,527 --> 00:09:46,025
could be funny too.

285
00:09:46,375 --> 00:09:48,522
And then, the best part of the

286
00:09:48,522 --> 00:09:48,952
night.

287
00:09:49,132 --> 00:09:51,955
Coming home, cheeks red from the cold,

288
00:09:51,955 --> 00:09:54,333
and dumping the whole bag out on

289
00:09:54,333 --> 00:09:55,819
the living room rug.

290
00:09:55,999 --> 00:09:58,321
Sorting the candy into piles.

291
00:09:58,501 --> 00:09:59,585
The good stuff,

292
00:09:59,585 --> 00:10:00,669
the okay stuff,

293
00:10:00,669 --> 00:10:02,692
and the stuff nobody wanted.

294
00:10:02,872 --> 00:10:06,037
Then trading with your brothers and sisters,

295
00:10:06,037 --> 00:10:08,483
like Wall Street traders on a busy

296
00:10:08,483 --> 00:10:08,770
day.

297
00:10:09,120 --> 00:10:11,717
Mothers usually took a look through it

298
00:10:11,717 --> 00:10:14,314
all, and fathers somehow ended up with

299
00:10:14,314 --> 00:10:16,295
a few of the best candy bars.

300
00:10:16,475 --> 00:10:18,736
Kids ate far too much before bed,

301
00:10:18,736 --> 00:10:20,996
and woke up the next morning with

302
00:10:20,996 --> 00:10:21,955
a stomachache.

303
00:10:22,135 --> 00:10:24,103
The candy lasted for weeks,

304
00:10:24,103 --> 00:10:25,707
or for about two days,

305
00:10:25,707 --> 00:10:27,383
depending on the child.

306
00:10:27,734 --> 00:10:29,020
And on November first,

307
00:10:29,020 --> 00:10:31,066
the town looked a little different.

308
00:10:31,246 --> 00:10:33,277
Smashed pumpkins in the street.

309
00:10:33,457 --> 00:10:36,468
Toilet paper hanging from a tree or

310
00:10:36,468 --> 00:10:36,813
two.

311
00:10:36,993 --> 00:10:38,920
Soap on a few windows.

312
00:10:39,100 --> 00:10:41,406
The big kids had still found time

313
00:10:41,406 --> 00:10:42,525
for some tricks.

314
00:10:42,705 --> 00:10:43,608
And at school,

315
00:10:43,608 --> 00:10:45,478
everyone compared their haul,

316
00:10:45,478 --> 00:10:47,929
and argued about which street had been

317
00:10:47,929 --> 00:10:48,510
the best.

318
00:10:48,859 --> 00:10:50,253
So what changed?

319
00:10:50,433 --> 00:10:52,795
In the late sixties and seventies,

320
00:10:52,795 --> 00:10:55,088
the neighborhood began to change.

321
00:10:55,268 --> 00:10:57,439
Fewer people knew all their neighbors.

322
00:10:57,619 --> 00:10:59,187
And scary news stories,

323
00:10:59,187 --> 00:11:01,572
many of them rumors that were never

324
00:11:01,572 --> 00:11:04,980
proven, convinced parents that homemade treats and

325
00:11:04,980 --> 00:11:07,093
unwrapped candy were dangerous.

326
00:11:07,273 --> 00:11:11,638
Popcorn balls and fudge disappeared almost overnight.

327
00:11:11,988 --> 00:11:15,199
Researchers who later looked into those stories

328
00:11:15,199 --> 00:11:17,863
found almost no real cases of strangers

329
00:11:17,863 --> 00:11:19,639
poisoning Halloween candy.

330
00:11:19,819 --> 00:11:21,131
But the fear stuck.

331
00:11:21,311 --> 00:11:23,251
By the nineteen eighties,

332
00:11:23,251 --> 00:11:24,957
it was mostly wrapped,

333
00:11:24,957 --> 00:11:26,431
factory-made candy.

334
00:11:26,611 --> 00:11:28,492
Parents walked with their kids.

335
00:11:28,672 --> 00:11:32,144
Hospitals offered to X-ray candy bags.

336
00:11:32,324 --> 00:11:35,127
And Halloween slowly moved from the street

337
00:11:35,127 --> 00:11:37,330
to the church parking lot and the

338
00:11:37,330 --> 00:11:37,664
mall.

339
00:11:38,014 --> 00:11:41,268
Today, Americans spend more than ten billion

340
00:11:41,268 --> 00:11:43,634
dollars on Halloween every year.

341
00:11:43,814 --> 00:11:47,993
Costumes, decorations and candy fill whole stores.

342
00:11:48,173 --> 00:11:50,716
Grown-ups dress up as much as kids.

343
00:11:50,896 --> 00:11:53,111
But I'd bet that for many of

344
00:11:53,111 --> 00:11:56,592
you, no store-bought Halloween will ever top

345
00:11:56,592 --> 00:11:57,938
a bedsheet ghost,

346
00:11:57,938 --> 00:12:00,548
a pillowcase, a porch light and a

347
00:12:00,548 --> 00:12:01,577
popcorn ball.

348
00:12:01,927 --> 00:12:05,085
Halloween in nineteen fifty-eight was about freedom.

349
00:12:05,265 --> 00:12:06,176
For one night,

350
00:12:06,176 --> 00:12:07,866
kids ran the neighborhood.

351
00:12:08,046 --> 00:12:09,055
They were brave,

352
00:12:09,055 --> 00:12:10,065
a little scared,

353
00:12:10,065 --> 00:12:11,390
and completely happy.

354
00:12:11,570 --> 00:12:14,275
They knew every street and every house.

355
00:12:14,455 --> 00:12:16,530
And every grown-up on the block was

356
00:12:16,530 --> 00:12:17,183
part of it,

357
00:12:17,183 --> 00:12:19,436
turning on the porch light and filling

358
00:12:19,436 --> 00:12:19,970
the bowl.

359
00:12:20,319 --> 00:12:22,235
It was also about trust.

360
00:12:22,415 --> 00:12:24,595
Neighbors trusted each other's kids,

361
00:12:24,595 --> 00:12:26,653
and parents trusted the neighbors.

362
00:12:26,833 --> 00:12:29,271
That trust is what made it possible

363
00:12:29,271 --> 00:12:32,127
for a seven-year-old in a cardboard robot

364
00:12:32,127 --> 00:12:34,286
suit to walk up to a stranger's

365
00:12:34,286 --> 00:12:36,376
door in the dark and come home

366
00:12:36,376 --> 00:12:38,396
safe, with a bag of candy and

367
00:12:38,396 --> 00:12:39,511
a story to tell.

368
00:12:39,860 --> 00:12:41,672
So tell us in the comments.

369
00:12:41,852 --> 00:12:44,562
What was your favorite Halloween costume as

370
00:12:44,562 --> 00:12:46,326
a kid, and what was the best

371
00:12:46,326 --> 00:12:48,028
house in your neighborhood?

372
00:12:48,208 --> 00:12:50,526
Did you carry a pillowcase or a

373
00:12:50,526 --> 00:12:51,273
paper bag?

374
00:12:51,453 --> 00:12:52,916
We read every comment.

375
00:12:53,096 --> 00:12:56,068
Happy Halloween, from all of us here.

376
00:12:56,248 --> 00:12:58,006
And thank you for spending this time

377
00:12:58,006 --> 00:12:58,396
with me.
